> &mdash; pehle AI era ki baat, phir contrast dikhaya ke schools kyun peeche hain &mdash; yeh reader ko baandhta hai.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p><strong>Problem ko visually dikhaya<\\/strong> &mdash; fees structure, curriculum waghera ko colored tiles mein dikhaya taake readers instantly samjhein.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p><strong>Skoolyst ka introduction<\\/strong> &mdash; problem ke baad naturally aata hai, sales-y nahi lagta.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p><strong>Clear call-to-action<\\/strong> &mdash; end mein green box mein register karne ka invitation, link ke saath.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Agar aap chahein toh main yeh bhi kar sakta hoon:<\\/p>\\n\\n<ul>\\n\\t<li>Is blog post ko copy-paste karne ke liye plain text format mein de sakta hoon<\\/li>\\n\\t<li>Social media caption (Instagram\\/Facebook) bhi likh sakta hoon is ke liye<\\/li>\\n\\t<li>Alag posts ke liye bhi help kar sakta hoon<\\/li>\\n<\\/ul>\\n\\n<p>&nbsp;<\\/p>\\n\"},\"position\":7,\"created_at\":\"2026-05-04T21:28:29.769092Z\"}]', '[]', NULL, '[{\"id\":\"element_1\",\"type\":\"heading\",\"content\":{\"text\":\"Hum AI aur Automation ke Zamane mein Hain \\u2014 Phir Schools Kyun Peeche Hain?\",\"level\":\"h2\"},\"position\":0,\"created_at\":\"2026-05-04T21:28:29.768814Z\"},{\"id\":\"element_2\",\"type\":\"text\",\"content\":{\"content\":\"<p>Socho zara &mdash; jab ghar bethe koi product kharidna ho, toh hum e-commerce sites pe jaate hain. Gaari chahiye? Centralized platforms hain. Information chahiye? Google hai. Har field apne aap ko update kar chuka hai &mdash; except ek jagah:&nbsp;<strong>schools.<\\/strong><\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Aaj bhi parents ko school dhundhne ke liye gali gali ghoomna padta hai. Street banners dekh ke decide karna padta hai. Aur phir bhi poori information nahi milti. Yeh 2025 ki haqeeqat hai &mdash; aur yeh accept karna mushkil hai.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Sochne wali baat:<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Ek street banner pe sirf school ka naam aur address likhna &mdash; kya yeh kaafi hai aaj ke digital parent ko convince karne ke liye?<\\/p>\\n\"},\"position\":1,\"created_at\":\"2026-05-04T21:28:29.768971Z\"},{\"id\":\"element_3\",\"type\":\"heading\",\"content\":{\"text\":\"Personal School Website \\u2014 Achha Idea, Lekin Ek Badi Problem\",\"level\":\"h2\"},\"position\":2,\"created_at\":\"2026-05-04T21:28:29.768997Z\"},{\"id\":\"element_4\",\"type\":\"text\",\"content\":{\"content\":\"<p>Kai school owners ne socha: &quot;Apna website banaate hain!&quot; &mdash; aur yeh soch buri nahi. Lekin problem yahan se shuru hoti hai:&nbsp;<em>har school ka alag website, alag design, alag layout.<\\/em><\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Jab parents kisi school ko dhundhte hain, toh unhe pehle website dhundhni padti hai. Phir us website pe navigate karna padta hai. Aur phir bhi yeh information aksar nahi milti:<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Fees Structure<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Facilities<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Curriculum<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Faculty Strength<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Student Strength<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Location &amp; Address<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Established Year<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Admin Details<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Yeh woh information hai jo har parent jaanna chahta hai &mdash; aur yeh ek hi jagah milni chahiye. Yahi problem thi. Aur isi problem ka jawab hai&nbsp;<strong>Skoolyst.<\\/strong><\\/p>\\n\"},\"position\":3,\"created_at\":\"2026-05-04T21:28:29.769019Z\"},{\"id\":\"element_5\",\"type\":\"heading\",\"content\":{\"text\":\"Skoolyst \\u2014 Problem Ka Real Solution\",\"level\":\"h2\"},\"position\":4,\"created_at\":\"2026-05-04T21:28:29.769038Z\"},{\"id\":\"element_6\",\"type\":\"text\",\"content\":{\"content\":\"<p>Skoolyst ek centralized digital platform hai &mdash; jahan pe schools apni poori details list kar sakti hain, aur parents ek hi jagah se sabhi schools ki compare kar sakte hain. Bilkul waise jaise aap e-commerce pe products compare karte hain.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Skoolyst pe aap kya kar sakte hain?<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>School register karein &mdash; basic info, email aur password se shuru karein<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Dashboard mein logo, cover image, gallery aur additional details add karein<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Publish status active karein &mdash; aur apni school Skoolyst ki list mein show ho jaye<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Articles likhein &mdash; parents aur students ko guide karein school choose karne ke baare mein<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Parents reviews aur feedback de sakein &mdash; school ki credibility badhe<\\/p>\\n\"},\"position\":5,\"created_at\":\"2026-05-04T21:28:29.769056Z\"},{\"id\":\"element_7\",\"type\":\"heading\",\"content\":{\"text\":\"Parents Ke Liye Bhi Fayda\",\"level\":\"h2\"},\"position\":6,\"created_at\":\"2026-05-04T21:28:29.769074Z\"},{\"id\":\"element_8\",\"type\":\"text\",\"content\":{\"content\":\"<p>Sirf schools hi nahi &mdash; parents bhi Skoolyst ka hissa bain sakte hain. Woh listed schools ke baare mein honest reviews de sakte hain, doosre parents ki madad kar sakte hain, aur platform ko improve karne ke liye feedback form bhar sakte hain&nbsp;<a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/skoolyst.com\\/\\\">skoolyst.com<\\/a>&nbsp;pe. Yahi community spirit Skoolyst ko sirf ek directory se zyada banata hai &mdash; yeh ek living, breathing education network hai.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Aaj hi apni school register karein<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Agar aap school owner hain, toh intezaar mat karein. Skoolyst pe apni school list karein, parents tak pahunchein, aur ek strong school community ka hissa banein.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p><a href=\\\"https:\\/\\/skoolyst.com\\/\\\">skoolyst.com pe jaayein<\\/a><\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Kya aap is topic pe apni raay dena chahte hain? Neeche feedback form fill karein ya humse rabta karein skoolyst.com pe.<br \\/>\\n&nbsp;<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Yeh rahaa aapka professional Roman Urdu blog post! Yahan kuch cheezein hain jo is post ko engaging banati hain:<\\/p>\\n\\n<p><strong>Title<\\/strong> &mdash; curiosity-driven hai, seedha question puchha hai jo reader ke zehan mein hoga.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p><strong>Opening hook<\\/strong> &mdash; pehle AI era ki baat, phir contrast dikhaya ke schools kyun peeche hain &mdash; yeh reader ko baandhta hai.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p><strong>Problem ko visually dikhaya<\\/strong> &mdash; fees structure, curriculum waghera ko colored tiles mein dikhaya taake readers instantly samjhein.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p><strong>Skoolyst ka introduction<\\/strong> &mdash; problem ke baad naturally aata hai, sales-y nahi lagta.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p><strong>Clear call-to-action<\\/strong> &mdash; end mein green box mein register karne ka invitation, link ke saath.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Agar aap chahein toh main yeh bhi kar sakta hoon:<\\/p>\\n\\n<ul>\\n\\t<li>Is blog post ko copy-paste karne ke liye plain text format mein de sakta hoon<\\/li>\\n\\t<li>Social media caption (Instagram\\/Facebook) bhi likh sakta hoon is ke liye<\\/li>\\n\\t<li>Alag posts ke liye bhi help kar sakta hoon<\\/li>\\n<\\/ul>\\n\\n<p>&nbsp;<\\/p>\\n\"},\"position\":7,\"created_at\":\"2026-05-04T21:28:29.769092Z\"}]', '2026-05-04 21:28:30', '2026-09-09 23:11:35'),
(16, 'b02bc3e7-d8da-42db-a6a1-0a5e9b5702fe', '[{\"id\":\"element_1\",\"type\":\"heading\",\"content\":{\"text\":\"Who I Am\",\"level\":\"h2\"},\"position\":0,\"created_at\":\"2026-08-24T20:43:57.510259Z\"}]', 2, 1, 2, 'Pages of Hope and Hard Work', 'pages-of-hope-and-hard-work', '{\"elements\":[{\"id\":\"element_1\",\"type\":\"heading\",\"content\":{\"text\":\"Who I Am\",\"level\":\"h2\"},\"position\":0,\"languageDirection\":\"ltr\"},{\"id\":\"element_2\",\"type\":\"text\",\"content\":{\"content\":\"<p>&nbsp;My name is <strong>Alishba <\\/strong>. I am a student, a writer, and a dreamer. I would describe myself as simple, kind, creative, and thoughtful. I love writing because it gives me a way to express the things I cannot always say out loud. I have dreams for my future, especially my dream of joining the Pakistan Army and studying at a top university. But behind these dreams is a normal student who also has doubts, fears, pressure, confusion, and difficult days. My journey is not only about achievements; it is also about everything I have experienced while trying to reach my goals.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p><strong>My educational journey<\\/strong> has been an important part of who I am. I completed my matric from Hack Public High School, and completed my second year from Government Girls Degree College, . Like many students, my studies have come with pressure, expectations, difficult days, and moments when I questioned whether I was doing enough. Sometimes I felt behind when I saw other students moving forward. I compared myself with others and wondered if I was on the right path. These feelings became an important part of my personal journey and helped me understand myself better.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>One of the <strong>biggest parts of my journey<\\/strong> has been my entry-test experience. I attempt the tests of Nust , Ned and mehran university and also aviation training hub .<br \\/>\\nI went through preparation, expectations, attempts, results, disappointment, rejection, and the decision to try again. Every test brought different emotions and different lessons. There were moments when I felt confident, and there were moments when I questioned myself. Seeing a result that was not what I hoped for was difficult, but it also taught me that one result cannot define my ability, my future, or the value of my effort. My journey included trying again and continuing to look for opportunities instead of allowing one setback to become the end of my story.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Throughout this journey, I have had many questions in my mind. I asked myself, &ldquo;Am I doing enough?&rdquo; &ldquo;Am I good enough?&rdquo; &ldquo;Why do I feel behind?&rdquo; &ldquo;What if I fail again?&rdquo; and &ldquo;Will I really achieve my dreams?&rdquo; Sometimes the biggest pressure was not from other people but from myself. I expected myself to do everything perfectly and felt guilty whenever I thought I was not moving fast enough. Slowly, I started learning that progress does not always look big. Sometimes simply getting up, studying again, trying again, learning from a mistake, or taking one small step is also progress.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>This journey also taught me that failure and rejection are not the end. They can hurt, disappoint us, and make us question our decisions, but they can also teach us things that success cannot. I learned to look at my mistakes, understand what I could improve, and continue working toward my goals. I also learned that everyone&#39;s timeline is different. I may not always be where I want to be, but that does not mean I am not moving forward.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>My love for writing eventually became connected with all of these experiences. I wanted a place where I could write about my real journey &mdash; not only the happy moments, but also the doubts, struggles, pressure, failures, small victories, hopes, and lessons. That is how the idea of Pages of Hope and Hard Work became meaningful to me. It is more than just a page or a social-media project. It is a collection of thoughts and experiences from a student who is still learning, still dreaming, and still trying.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>The idea behind Pages of Hope and Hard Work is to reach students who may be going through something similar. A student preparing for an entry test, waiting for a result, facing rejection, feeling behind, losing confidence, or simply wondering what to do next may feel like they are the only one struggling. Through my writing, I want them to know that they are not alone. I do not want to pretend that I have all the answers. I simply want to share my own experiences honestly and show that difficult phases can become part of a bigger journey.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>My book idea also comes from the same purpose. I want to collect my experiences, thoughts, lessons, dreams, failures, hopes, and memories in one place. I want the book to represent the real journey behind the pages &mdash; the person I was, the person I am becoming, and the dreams I am still working toward. I want it to be something students can read and relate to, especially during the moments when they feel uncertain about themselves or their future.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>My journey is still not complete. I am still a student, still learning, still working on myself, and still chasing my dreams. I still have questions, and I still have days when I doubt myself. But now I understand that I do not need to have everything figured out at once. I can keep learning, keep improving, keep working, and keep moving forward one step at a time.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>If there is one message I want students to take from my journey, it is this: your result is only one page of your story, not the whole book. A failure does not erase your hard work, a rejection does not erase your dreams, and feeling behind does not mean you have stopped moving. Keep hope, keep working, keep learning from every experience, and keep believing that your story can still take you somewhere beautiful. My story is still being written &mdash; and these are my Pages of Hope and Hard Work.<\\/p>\\n\"},\"position\":1,\"languageDirection\":\"ltr\"}],\"metadata\":{\"created\":\"2026-08-24T20:43:47.650Z\",\"total_elements\":2,\"version\":\"1.0\"}}', 'A student\'s honest journey through entry-test rejections, self-doubt, and the belief that one result doesn\'t define your future. Alishba shares her real story of failure, hope, and moving forward.\r\n\r\n\r\nStudent Stories, Entry Test Journey, Motivation, Failure and Growth, Pages of Hope and Hard Work, Student Success,', '<h2>Who I Am</h2><p>&nbsp;My name is <strong>Alishba </strong>. I am a student, a writer, and a dreamer. I would describe myself as simple, kind, creative, and thoughtful. I love writing because it gives me a way to express the things I cannot always say out loud. I have dreams for my future, especially my dream of joining the Pakistan Army and studying at a top university. But behind these dreams is a normal student who also has doubts, fears, pressure, confusion, and difficult days. My journey is not only about achievements; it is also about everything I have experienced while trying to reach my goals.</p>\n\n<p><strong>My educational journey</strong> has been an important part of who I am. I completed my matric from Hack Public High School, and completed my second year from Government Girls Degree College, . Like many students, my studies have come with pressure, expectations, difficult days, and moments when I questioned whether I was doing enough. Sometimes I felt behind when I saw other students moving forward. I compared myself with others and wondered if I was on the right path. These feelings became an important part of my personal journey and helped me understand myself better.</p>\n\n<p>One of the <strong>biggest parts of my journey</strong> has been my entry-test experience. I attempt the tests of Nust , Ned and mehran university and also aviation training hub .<br />\nI went through preparation, expectations, attempts, results, disappointment, rejection, and the decision to try again. Every test brought different emotions and different lessons. There were moments when I felt confident, and there were moments when I questioned myself. Seeing a result that was not what I hoped for was difficult, but it also taught me that one result cannot define my ability, my future, or the value of my effort. My journey included trying again and continuing to look for opportunities instead of allowing one setback to become the end of my story.</p>\n\n<p>Throughout this journey, I have had many questions in my mind. I asked myself, &ldquo;Am I doing enough?&rdquo; &ldquo;Am I good enough?&rdquo; &ldquo;Why do I feel behind?&rdquo; &ldquo;What if I fail again?&rdquo; and &ldquo;Will I really achieve my dreams?&rdquo; Sometimes the biggest pressure was not from other people but from myself. I expected myself to do everything perfectly and felt guilty whenever I thought I was not moving fast enough. Slowly, I started learning that progress does not always look big. Sometimes simply getting up, studying again, trying again, learning from a mistake, or taking one small step is also progress.</p>\n\n<p>This journey also taught me that failure and rejection are not the end. They can hurt, disappoint us, and make us question our decisions, but they can also teach us things that success cannot. I learned to look at my mistakes, understand what I could improve, and continue working toward my goals. I also learned that everyone&#39;s timeline is different. I may not always be where I want to be, but that does not mean I am not moving forward.</p>\n\n<p>My love for writing eventually became connected with all of these experiences. I wanted a place where I could write about my real journey &mdash; not only the happy moments, but also the doubts, struggles, pressure, failures, small victories, hopes, and lessons. That is how the idea of Pages of Hope and Hard Work became meaningful to me. It is more than just a page or a social-media project. It is a collection of thoughts and experiences from a student who is still learning, still dreaming, and still trying.</p>\n\n<p>The idea behind Pages of Hope and Hard Work is to reach students who may be going through something similar. A student preparing for an entry test, waiting for a result, facing rejection, feeling behind, losing confidence, or simply wondering what to do next may feel like they are the only one struggling. Through my writing, I want them to know that they are not alone. I do not want to pretend that I have all the answers. I simply want to share my own experiences honestly and show that difficult phases can become part of a bigger journey.</p>\n\n<p>My book idea also comes from the same purpose. I want to collect my experiences, thoughts, lessons, dreams, failures, hopes, and memories in one place. I want the book to represent the real journey behind the pages &mdash; the person I was, the person I am becoming, and the dreams I am still working toward. I want it to be something students can read and relate to, especially during the moments when they feel uncertain about themselves or their future.</p>\n\n<p>My journey is still not complete. I am still a student, still learning, still working on myself, and still chasing my dreams. I still have questions, and I still have days when I doubt myself. But now I understand that I do not need to have everything figured out at once. I can keep learning, keep improving, keep working, and keep moving forward one step at a time.</p>\n\n<p>If there is one message I want students to take from my journey, it is this: your result is only one page of your story, not the whole book. A failure does not erase your hard work, a rejection does not erase your dreams, and feeling behind does not mean you have stopped moving. Keep hope, keep working, keep learning from every experience, and keep believing that your story can still take you somewhere beautiful. My story is still being written &mdash; and these are my Pages of Hope and Hard Work.</p>\n', 'blog/featured-images/5ff21a6a-7036-4b65-b6f7-531111e00a20.webp', '[\"Student\",\"Stories\",\"EntryTestJourney\",\"Motivation\",\"FailureAndGrowth\",\"PagesOfHopeAndHardWork\",\"StudentSuccess\"]', 224, 50.24330, 5, 'published', 1, '2026-08-24 01:20:00', 'Pages of Hope and Hard Work | A Student\'s Journey of Failure & Hope — Skoolyst', 'Alishba, a student preparing for NUST, NED, and Mehran University entry tests, shares her honest story of rejection, self-doubt, and learning that failure isn\'t the end of the road.', '[]', '[]', '[{\"id\":\"element_2\",\"type\":\"text\",\"content\":{\"content\":\"<p>&nbsp;My name is <strong>Alishba <\\/strong>. I am a student, a writer, and a dreamer. I would describe myself as simple, kind, creative, and thoughtful. I love writing because it gives me a way to express the things I cannot always say out loud. I have dreams for my future, especially my dream of joining the Pakistan Army and studying at a top university. But behind these dreams is a normal student who also has doubts, fears, pressure, confusion, and difficult days. My journey is not only about achievements; it is also about everything I have experienced while trying to reach my goals.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p><strong>My educational journey<\\/strong> has been an important part of who I am. I completed my matric from Hack Public High School, and completed my second year from Government Girls Degree College, . Like many students, my studies have come with pressure, expectations, difficult days, and moments when I questioned whether I was doing enough. Sometimes I felt behind when I saw other students moving forward. I compared myself with others and wondered if I was on the right path. These feelings became an important part of my personal journey and helped me understand myself better.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>One of the <strong>biggest parts of my journey<\\/strong> has been my entry-test experience. I attempt the tests of Nust , Ned and mehran university and also aviation training hub .<br \\/>\\nI went through preparation, expectations, attempts, results, disappointment, rejection, and the decision to try again. Every test brought different emotions and different lessons. There were moments when I felt confident, and there were moments when I questioned myself. Seeing a result that was not what I hoped for was difficult, but it also taught me that one result cannot define my ability, my future, or the value of my effort. My journey included trying again and continuing to look for opportunities instead of allowing one setback to become the end of my story.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Throughout this journey, I have had many questions in my mind. I asked myself, &ldquo;Am I doing enough?&rdquo; &ldquo;Am I good enough?&rdquo; &ldquo;Why do I feel behind?&rdquo; &ldquo;What if I fail again?&rdquo; and &ldquo;Will I really achieve my dreams?&rdquo; Sometimes the biggest pressure was not from other people but from myself. I expected myself to do everything perfectly and felt guilty whenever I thought I was not moving fast enough. Slowly, I started learning that progress does not always look big. Sometimes simply getting up, studying again, trying again, learning from a mistake, or taking one small step is also progress.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>This journey also taught me that failure and rejection are not the end. They can hurt, disappoint us, and make us question our decisions, but they can also teach us things that success cannot. I learned to look at my mistakes, understand what I could improve, and continue working toward my goals. I also learned that everyone&#39;s timeline is different. I may not always be where I want to be, but that does not mean I am not moving forward.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>My love for writing eventually became connected with all of these experiences. I wanted a place where I could write about my real journey &mdash; not only the happy moments, but also the doubts, struggles, pressure, failures, small victories, hopes, and lessons. That is how the idea of Pages of Hope and Hard Work became meaningful to me. It is more than just a page or a social-media project. It is a collection of thoughts and experiences from a student who is still learning, still dreaming, and still trying.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>The idea behind Pages of Hope and Hard Work is to reach students who may be going through something similar. A student preparing for an entry test, waiting for a result, facing rejection, feeling behind, losing confidence, or simply wondering what to do next may feel like they are the only one struggling. Through my writing, I want them to know that they are not alone. I do not want to pretend that I have all the answers. I simply want to share my own experiences honestly and show that difficult phases can become part of a bigger journey.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>My book idea also comes from the same purpose. I want to collect my experiences, thoughts, lessons, dreams, failures, hopes, and memories in one place. I want the book to represent the real journey behind the pages &mdash; the person I was, the person I am becoming, and the dreams I am still working toward. I want it to be something students can read and relate to, especially during the moments when they feel uncertain about themselves or their future.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>My journey is still not complete. I am still a student, still learning, still working on myself, and still chasing my dreams. I still have questions, and I still have days when I doubt myself. But now I understand that I do not need to have everything figured out at once. I can keep learning, keep improving, keep working, and keep moving forward one step at a time.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>If there is one message I want students to take from my journey, it is this: your result is only one page of your story, not the whole book. A failure does not erase your hard work, a rejection does not erase your dreams, and feeling behind does not mean you have stopped moving. Keep hope, keep working, keep learning from every experience, and keep believing that your story can still take you somewhere beautiful. My story is still being written &mdash; and these are my Pages of Hope and Hard Work.<\\/p>\\n\"},\"position\":1,\"created_at\":\"2026-08-24T20:43:57.510400Z\"}]', '[]', NULL, '[{\"id\":\"element_1\",\"type\":\"heading\",\"content\":{\"text\":\"Who I Am\",\"level\":\"h2\"},\"position\":0,\"created_at\":\"2026-08-24T20:43:57.510259Z\"},{\"id\":\"element_2\",\"type\":\"text\",\"content\":{\"content\":\"<p>&nbsp;My name is <strong>Alishba <\\/strong>. I am a student, a writer, and a dreamer. I would describe myself as simple, kind, creative, and thoughtful. I love writing because it gives me a way to express the things I cannot always say out loud. I have dreams for my future, especially my dream of joining the Pakistan Army and studying at a top university. But behind these dreams is a normal student who also has doubts, fears, pressure, confusion, and difficult days. My journey is not only about achievements; it is also about everything I have experienced while trying to reach my goals.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p><strong>My educational journey<\\/strong> has been an important part of who I am. I completed my matric from Hack Public High School, and completed my second year from Government Girls Degree College, . Like many students, my studies have come with pressure, expectations, difficult days, and moments when I questioned whether I was doing enough. Sometimes I felt behind when I saw other students moving forward. I compared myself with others and wondered if I was on the right path. These feelings became an important part of my personal journey and helped me understand myself better.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>One of the <strong>biggest parts of my journey<\\/strong> has been my entry-test experience. I attempt the tests of Nust , Ned and mehran university and also aviation training hub .<br \\/>\\nI went through preparation, expectations, attempts, results, disappointment, rejection, and the decision to try again. Every test brought different emotions and different lessons. There were moments when I felt confident, and there were moments when I questioned myself. Seeing a result that was not what I hoped for was difficult, but it also taught me that one result cannot define my ability, my future, or the value of my effort. My journey included trying again and continuing to look for opportunities instead of allowing one setback to become the end of my story.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>Throughout this journey, I have had many questions in my mind. I asked myself, &ldquo;Am I doing enough?&rdquo; &ldquo;Am I good enough?&rdquo; &ldquo;Why do I feel behind?&rdquo; &ldquo;What if I fail again?&rdquo; and &ldquo;Will I really achieve my dreams?&rdquo; Sometimes the biggest pressure was not from other people but from myself. I expected myself to do everything perfectly and felt guilty whenever I thought I was not moving fast enough. Slowly, I started learning that progress does not always look big. Sometimes simply getting up, studying again, trying again, learning from a mistake, or taking one small step is also progress.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>This journey also taught me that failure and rejection are not the end. They can hurt, disappoint us, and make us question our decisions, but they can also teach us things that success cannot. I learned to look at my mistakes, understand what I could improve, and continue working toward my goals. I also learned that everyone&#39;s timeline is different. I may not always be where I want to be, but that does not mean I am not moving forward.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>My love for writing eventually became connected with all of these experiences. I wanted a place where I could write about my real journey &mdash; not only the happy moments, but also the doubts, struggles, pressure, failures, small victories, hopes, and lessons. That is how the idea of Pages of Hope and Hard Work became meaningful to me. It is more than just a page or a social-media project. It is a collection of thoughts and experiences from a student who is still learning, still dreaming, and still trying.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>The idea behind Pages of Hope and Hard Work is to reach students who may be going through something similar. A student preparing for an entry test, waiting for a result, facing rejection, feeling behind, losing confidence, or simply wondering what to do next may feel like they are the only one struggling. Through my writing, I want them to know that they are not alone. I do not want to pretend that I have all the answers. I simply want to share my own experiences honestly and show that difficult phases can become part of a bigger journey.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>My book idea also comes from the same purpose. I want to collect my experiences, thoughts, lessons, dreams, failures, hopes, and memories in one place. I want the book to represent the real journey behind the pages &mdash; the person I was, the person I am becoming, and the dreams I am still working toward. I want it to be something students can read and relate to, especially during the moments when they feel uncertain about themselves or their future.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>My journey is still not complete. I am still a student, still learning, still working on myself, and still chasing my dreams. I still have questions, and I still have days when I doubt myself. But now I understand that I do not need to have everything figured out at once. I can keep learning, keep improving, keep working, and keep moving forward one step at a time.<\\/p>\\n\\n<p>If there is one message I want students to take from my journey, it is this: your result is only one page of your story, not the whole book. A failure does not erase your hard work, a rejection does not erase your dreams, and feeling behind does not mean you have stopped moving. Keep hope, keep working, keep learning from every experience, and keep believing that your story can still take you somewhere beautiful. My story is still being written &mdash; and these are my Pages of Hope and Hard Work.<\\/p>\\n\"},\"position\":1,\"created_at\":\"2026-08-24T20:43:57.510400Z\"}]', '2026-08-24 20:34:37', '2026-09-10 17:37:14');
